# Title II — Committee on Armed Services

**Sec. 20001. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for improving the quality of life for military personnel.**
This section appropriates roughly $7.9 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for troop quality-of-life programs, including $2.9 billion to supplement the Basic Allowance for Housing, $2 billion for the Defense Health Program, $1 billion for unaccompanied housing modernization, $100 million each for tuition assistance and child care fee assistance, $590 million to extend Temporary Lodging Expense to 21 days, and $230.48 million for Marine Corps Barracks 2030. It also temporarily raises the authorized investment percentage for privatized military housing projects from 33⅓/45 percent to 60 percent through September 30, 2029. Finally, it expands 10 U.S.C. § 2881a to let any "Secretary concerned" (not just the Navy) contract with private-sector entities for privatized unaccompanied housing, extending the authority to September 30, 2029.

**Sec. 20002. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for shipbuilding.**
This section appropriates approximately $29 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for shipbuilding and the naval industrial base. The largest items are $4.6 billion for a second Virginia-class submarine in FY2026, $5.4 billion for two additional DDG guided-missile destroyers, $2.725 billion for T-AO oilers, $1.803941 billion for Landing Ship Medium procurement, and $1.47 billion for a multi-ship amphibious warship contract. It also funds industrial-base expansion, including $750 million for supplier development, $500 million each for advanced manufacturing and dry-dock capability, $2.1 billion for medium unmanned surface vessels, $1.3 billion for unmanned underwater vehicles, and $1.534 billion for small unmanned surface vessel production.

**Sec. 20003. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for integrated air and missile defense.**
This section appropriates about $22.8 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) in two parts. Subsection (a) provides $18.3 billion for next-generation missile defense technologies, including $5.6 billion for space-based and boost-phase intercept capabilities, $7.2 billion for space-based sensors, $2.55 billion for missile defense capabilities, $2 billion for air moving target indicator satellites, and $500 million for space launch infrastructure. Subsection (b) provides roughly $5.9 billion for layered homeland defense, including $2.2 billion for hypersonic defense acceleration, $1.975 billion for ground-based missile defense radars, $800 million for next-generation ICBM defense systems, and $530 million for a Missile Defense Agency instrumentation range safety ship.

**Sec. 20004. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for munitions and defense supply chain resiliency.**
This section appropriates over $20 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) across 67 line items for munitions and industrial-base resilience, including $1 billion each for the one-way attack unmanned aerial system industrial base and next-generation automated munitions production factories, $2 billion for critical minerals stockpiling via the National Defense Stockpile Transaction Fund, and $500 million for counter-UAS programs. Subsections (b) and (c) add $3.3 billion for Industrial Base Fund grants/purchase commitments and $5 billion for critical minerals supply chain investments under 10 U.S.C. § 4817. Subsection (d) provides $500 million to the Department of Defense Credit Program Account for loans, loan guarantees, and technical assistance for critical minerals industries, subsidizing up to $100 billion in loan principal under 10 U.S.C. § 149(e).

**Sec. 20005. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for scaling low-cost weapons into production.**
This section appropriates roughly $17 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) across 33 line items, including $2 billion for Defense Innovation Unit commercial technology scaling, $1.5 billion for low-cost cruise missiles, $1.4 billion for the small unmanned aerial system industrial base, $1.685 billion for military cryptographic modernization, and $1 billion each for innovative technology fielding and industrial-base qualification/competition activities. It also funds $750 million for military logistics and energy capabilities, $500 million for advanced 5G/6G military technologies, and $250 million each for AI ecosystem advancement, Cyber Command AI efforts, and the Quantum Benchmarking Initiative. Subsection (b) appropriates $1 billion to the Department of Defense Credit Program Account under 10 U.S.C. § 149(e) for capital assistance (loans, guarantees, technical assistance), subsidizing up to $100 billion in loan principal.

**Sec. 20006. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for improving the efficiency and cybersecurity of the Department of Defense.**
This section appropriates $380 million to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) in four line items. It provides $150 million for business systems replacement and $200 million for automation and artificial intelligence, both aimed at accelerating audits of DoD's financial statements under chapter 9A and section 2222 of title 10, U.S.C. It also provides $10 million for the Office of the Secretary of Defense's budgetary and programmatic infrastructure and $20 million for DARPA defense cybersecurity programs.

**Sec. 20007. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for air superiority.**
This section appropriates roughly $8.1 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) across 19 line items for Air Force and Navy airpower. The largest items are $3.15 billion to increase F-15EX production, $750 million to accelerate the FA/XX aircraft, $678 million for the Collaborative Combat Aircraft program, $600 million and $500 million for Air Force and Navy long-range strike aircraft respectively, and $474 million to increase EA-37B production. It also includes $361.22 million to prevent F-22 retirements, $127.46 million to prevent F-15E retirements, $400 million to accelerate F-47 production, and $440 million to increase C-130J production.

**Sec. 20008. Enhancement of resources for nuclear forces.**
Subsection (a) appropriates about $10.7 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029), including $4.5 billion for B-21 bomber production capacity expansion, $2.5 billion for Sentinel ICBM risk reduction, $2 billion for the nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missile, $500 million for Minuteman III improvements, and $400 million for Trident D5LE2 development. Subsection (b) appropriates about $3.9 billion to the Administrator of the National Nuclear Security Administration, including $1 billion to accelerate facility construction, $750 million each for primary and secondary capability modernization, $540 million for deferred maintenance, and $400 million for the sea-launched cruise missile warhead. It also funds $120 million for domestic uranium enrichment centrifuge deployment and $115 million for AI-enabled nuclear security missions.

**Sec. 20009. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources to improve capabilities of United States Indo-Pacific Command.**
This section appropriates roughly $13.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for Indo-Pacific and related priorities. Major items include $3.65 billion for development, procurement, integration, and protection of U.S. military satellites, $1.1 billion for Indo-Pacific infrastructure and $1 billion each for airfield development, offensive cyber operations, and the X-37B spacecraft program, plus $850 million for replenishment of military articles. It also funds $532.6 million for the Pacific Air Force biennial large-scale exercise, $528 million for DARC and SILENTBARKER space situational awareness, $200 million for the Guam Defense System, and smaller amounts for exercises by the Army ($365 million), Special Operations Command ($53 million), Marine Corps ($47 million), and Air Force ($90 million) in the Western Pacific.

**Sec. 20010. Enhancement of Department of Defense resources for improving the readiness of the Department of Defense.**
This section appropriates roughly $18.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) across 23 line items for readiness and maintenance. The largest items are $2.5 billion for Air Force facilities sustainment, restoration, and modernization; $2.118 billion for Air Force aircraft spares and repairs; $2 billion for Navy depot and shipyard modernization; $1.64 billion for Special Operations Command equipment and operations; and $1.5 billion each for Army depot modernization and increased depot/shipyard maintenance. It also provides $1.4 billion and $700 million for OPN-8 maritime spares pilot programs, $500 million for National Guard unit readiness, and $320 million for Joint Special Operations Command.

**Sec. 20011. Improving Department of Defense border support and counter-drug missions.**
This section appropriates $1 billion to the Secretary of Defense for FY2025 (available through September 30, 2029) for border and counter-drug operations. The funds cover deployment of military personnel for border operations, operations and maintenance supporting those missions, counter-narcotics and counter-transnational criminal organization support, operation of and construction in "national defense areas," and temporary detention of migrants on DoD installations. All activities must comply with chapter 15 of title 10, U.S.C. (the Insurrection Act–related authorities governing domestic military employment).

**Sec. 20012. Department of Defense oversight.**
This section appropriates $10 million to the Inspector General of the Department of Defense for FY2025, available through September 30, 2029. The funds are for monitoring DoD activities funded under this title, specifically programs with mutual technological dependencies, programs with data management and data ownership considerations, and programs vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and long lead-time components.

**Sec. 20013. Military construction projects authorized.**
Subsection (a) authorizes the appropriation of funds for military construction, land acquisition, and military family housing functions of each military department (as defined in 10 U.S.C. § 101(a)) as specified in this title. Subsection (b) requires the Secretary of each military department, within 30 days of enactment, to submit a detailed project-by-project spending plan for all funds made available by this title to the Senate and House Committees on Armed Services. [end of text]


